 in this movie to  the current covered 19 situation and  they grade the movie's high for focusing  on fomites as opposed to you know zombie  bites but they do point out that the  turnaround time would actually need to  be longer more than a few days for the  virus to shed from Beth's respiratory  tract or saliva tube spread to anything  she's touching the fictional disease in  contagion is called ma vie one Burns and  Soderbergh conceived it while  researching the 2009 h1n1 flu pandemic  the 2003 SARS outbreak and others they  consulted with researchers like Larry  brilliant  known for his work in eradicating  smallpox Ian Lipkin whu-oh specialist  and author Laurie Garrett people who  have dedicated their lives to studying  real outbreak scenarios and conceiving  hypothetical nightmare situations like  the one presented in this film Beth dies  two days later mev one moves super fast  and that's a big part of its danger  infected characters don't have much time  after exposure to reacts educate  themselves self quarantine etc before  they accidentally pass it on to someone  else  by comparison Kovac seems to have a  longer incubation period at least longer  than a week a lot more time to recognize  symptoms and do something about it  another big difference appears to be the  mortality rates MeV has a mortality rate  between twenty and thirty percent and by  the end of the movie ends up killing  twenty-six million people worldwide  that's insanely high but not as high as  the 50 million people who died in the  real-life 1918 Spanish flu but it's  still very high kovetz mortality rate is  still being determined but as of March  3rd it mentioned it at 3.4 percent of  reported cases but again that number may  have changed by the time you're watching  this so do your own research contagions  disaster response is roughly accurate to  the real world  the w-h-o deploys epidemiologist dr.  Arantes to hong kong to try to identify  its source meanwhile the CDC deploys CIS  officer dr. Mears to the cluster region  in Minneapolis to try to contain it and  while this happens we get more terms to  understand this disease for every person  who gets sick how many other people are  they likely to infect we call that  number B R naught R stands for the  reproductive rate of the virus so in  contagion the R naught of MeV one starts  at two but then later jumps to for the R  naught of Koba 19 is estimated right now  to be at 2 to 2.5 and again that doesn't  make it deadlier it's just a lot more  infectious than the seasonal flu that  comes every year the movie also  introduces CDC scientist dr. Hextall who  breaks down the MeV for us he's in  colors the virus contains both bat and  pig sequences  and pig back attaches to the cell like a  key slipping into a lock somewhere in  the world  the wrong people met up with the wrong  back indeed the final scene of the film  reveals those near accidental day1  events Beth's mining company de force  did a jungle displacing some bats that  eats banana banana chunk drops in pig  pen pig eats banana pig ends up in  kitchen chef touches pigs mouth chef  doesn't wash his hands affects winneth  when it kills everyone M eveyone in this  way is similar to the nipple virus in  Malaysia in the late 90s which was  believed to originate from bats  displaced by deforestation and was  spread to humans from pigs screenwriter  Byrnes researched eco tones humans  invade remote areas and exposed the food  chain to microbes human immune systems  aren't biologically prepared for and our  current disease actually stems from a  family of beta corona viruses that all  have their origins in bats okay so in  the race to develop a vaccine Hextall  knows that it'll take months and months  and months of human trials and FDA  approval so she takes the enormous risk  and tests a vaccine strain on herself  and luckily it works this is apparently  based on physicist Barry Marshall who  inoculated himself with Helicobacter  pylori to prove